(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacodynamics-exam-2-pharmacodynamics-master-exam"]={"title":"Pharmacodynamics Comprehensive Quiz — Set 1","category":"Pharmacodynamics","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"A drug moves across the endothelial lining of blood vessels through aqueous pores, down its concentration gradient. This process is best described by which principle?","choices":["Endocytosis of impermeant solutes","Carrier-mediated active transport","Fick's law of aqueous diffusion","Michaelis-Menten capacity-limited kinetics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aqueous diffusion through the endothelial pores between blood and extravascular space is governed by Fick's law, driven by the concentration gradient. — PK I & II, Slide 9-10"},{"q":"According to Fick's law, drug absorption is fastest across a membrane that is:","choices":["Large in surface area and thick","Large in surface area and thin","Small in surface area and thin","Small in surface area and thick"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flux rises with area and falls with thickness, so large area + thin membrane (e.g., lungs) maximizes absorption. — PK I & II, Slide 11"},{"q":"A weak acid drug is placed in an acidic environment. What is the consequence for its membrane permeability?","choices":["It becomes charged and water-soluble","It becomes deprotonated, charged, and lipid-insoluble","It becomes protonated, uncharged, and lipid-soluble","Its pKa shifts toward neutrality"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In an acidic solution a weak acid is protonated (HA), uncharged, and therefore lipid-soluble — as with the protonated form of aspirin. — PK I & II, Slide 18"},{"q":"P-glycoprotein (MDR1) is best characterized as a transporter that:","choices":["Passively moves lipophilic drugs down their concentration gradient","Governs Fick's-law diffusion in the gut","Only functions in renal tubular reabsorption","Actively effluxes substrates and can be inhibited by grapefruit juice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. MDR1/P-gp is a special efflux carrier in brain, testes, and GI tract; inhibiting it (e.g., grapefruit juice with statins) alters absorption of other drugs. — PK I & II, Slide 21"},{"q":"Volume of distribution (Vd) is best defined as:","choices":["The literal measured fluid volume of the vascular compartment alone","The apparent volume needed to account for the measured plasma concentration","The volume of drug metabolized per hour","The plasma volume cleared of drug per minute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vd is the apparent volume a drug would occupy to produce the measured serum concentration; it reflects tissue distribution. — PK I & II, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 50 mg dose of a lipophilic drug yields a plasma concentration of 0.5 mg/L. What is its Vd?","choices":["5 L","100 L","25 L","250 L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vd = D/C = 50 mg ÷ 0.5 mg/L = 100 L, consistent with extensive tissue distribution of a lipophilic drug. — PK I & II, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which change would be expected to DECREASE a drug's volume of distribution?","choices":["Extensive plasma protein binding","Renal failure with fluid retention","Liver failure altering body fluid","High lipophilicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High affinity for plasma proteins keeps more drug in plasma, reducing apparent Vd. — PK I & II, Slide 32"},{"q":"The primary purpose of drug metabolism (biotransformation) is to:","choices":["Increase the drug's lipid solubility for storage","Always convert the drug into a more active form","Make the drug more polar so it can be renally excreted","Bind the drug to plasma proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metabolism generally makes substances more polar so they can be filtered by the glomerulus and excreted. — PK I & II, Slide 34"},{"q":"Codeine converted to morphine is an example of:","choices":["A first-pass inactivation","A prodrug requiring metabolism for activity","A phase II glucuronide conjugation product","Zero-order elimination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Codeine is a prodrug metabolized to active morphine, like enalapril→enalaprilat. — PK I & II, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which is a defining feature of a phase I metabolic reaction?","choices":["Conjugation via glucuronidation","Sulfation to increase solubility","Direct renal filtration of the unchanged parent drug","Introducing or uncovering a polar functional group"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Phase I (oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis) introduces or uncovers polar groups that provide sites for phase II. — PK I & II, Slide 37"},{"q":"In the cytochrome P450 designation CYP2D6, the '2' refers to the:","choices":["Individual enzyme with a unique sequence","Subfamily (>55% homology)","Number of substrates it metabolizes","Family (>40% homology)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The first numeral denotes the family, defined by >40% amino acid homology. — PK I & II, Slide 46"},{"q":"The first-pass effect refers to:","choices":["Rapid renal clearance on the first filtration","Saturation of plasma protein binding sites","Reduction of drug concentration by hepatic metabolism before reaching systemic circulation","The initial rapid distribution phase occurring immediately after an intravenous injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug absorbed from the intestine passes via the portal system to the liver, where metabolism reduces the amount reaching systemic circulation. — PK I & II, Slide 43"},{"q":"Weak acids are excreted faster in alkaline urine because:","choices":["A greater fraction becomes charged, reducing tubular reabsorption","Alkaline urine keeps them uncharged and readily reabsorbed","Alkaline urine increases their lipid solubility","Alkalinization inhibits glomerular filtration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In alkaline urine a higher proportion of a weak acid is ionized/charged, so it is not reabsorbed and is excreted faster (basis for bicarbonate in aspirin overdose). — PK I & II, Slide 56"},{"q":"Total body clearance is best represented by which relationship?","choices":["ClTotal = ClRenal + ClLiver + ClBiliary + ClOther","ClTotal = Vd × Ke only","ClTotal = Dose / half-life","ClTotal = C1 − C2 measured across the membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Total clearance is the additive sum of clearance by each eliminating route. — PK I & II, Slide 58"},{"q":"Renal clearance is clinically estimated using creatinine clearance because creatinine is:","choices":["Actively secreted by the tubule and heavily reabsorbed","Produced at a constant rate by muscle and cleared by excretion","Bound extensively to plasma proteins","Metabolized primarily by CYP3A4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Creatinine is a muscle-metabolism product generated at a constant rate and cleared by excretion, making it a useful marker of renal function. — PK I & II, Slide 59"},{"q":"A drug eliminated by first-order kinetics has:","choices":["A constant amount removed per unit time","A constant percentage removed per unit time","Elimination independent of concentration","A fixed 20 mg/dL/hr elimination like ethanol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-order elimination removes a constant fraction/percentage per unit time and is proportional to concentration; most drugs follow this. — PK I & II, Slide 63"},{"q":"Ethanol metabolism at roughly 20 mg/dL/hr regardless of blood level illustrates:","choices":["First-order kinetics","Flip-flop kinetics","Zero-order (capacity-limited) kinetics","Concentration-dependent clearance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A constant amount eliminated per time, independent of concentration, defines zero-order/capacity-limited kinetics — ethanol is the classic example. — PK I & II, Slide 63"},{"q":"The elimination rate constant Ke is related to clearance and volume of distribution by:","choices":["Ke = Cl / Vd","Ke = Vd / Cl","Ke = Cl × Vd","Ke = 0.693 × Cl"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ke = Cl/Vd; it is the fraction of drug eliminated per unit time. — PK I & II, Slide 68"},{"q":"If a drug's plasma level falls from 20 mcg/mL to 10 mcg/mL over 4 hours, its half-life is:","choices":["2 hours","10 hours","8 hours","4 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The concentration halved (20→10) over exactly 4 hours, so t½ = 4 hours. — PK I & II, Slide 69"},{"q":"Given t½ = (0.693 × Vd)/Cl, a LARGER volume of distribution will:","choices":["Prolong the half-life","Shorten the half-life","Have no effect on half-life","Increase clearance proportionally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A larger Vd increases the numerator, prolonging half-life. — PK I & II, Slide 72-73"},{"q":"Steady state is defined as the point at which:","choices":["The rate of drug administration equals the rate of elimination","Plasma concentration first becomes reliably measurable","The loading dose has been fully absorbed","Clearance falls to zero"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At steady state, input rate equals elimination rate, so average plasma level plateaus. — PK I & II, Slide 74"},{"q":"Approximately how many half-lives are required to reach steady state?","choices":["1-2 half-lives","It depends only on the loading dose","8-10 half-lives","4-5 half-lives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Roughly 4-5 half-lives are needed to approach steady state. — PK I & II, Slide 75"},{"q":"A loading dose is used primarily to:","choices":["Slow down the approach toward steady state","Rapidly achieve a desired (target) plasma concentration","Reduce total clearance","Prevent first-pass metabolism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loading dose = Vd × target concentration; it promptly raises plasma level to the desired steady-state target. — PK I & II, Slide 80"},{"q":"Using Css = Dose/(Cl × dosing interval), increasing the dosing interval while holding dose constant will:","choices":["Decrease average steady-state concentration","Increase average steady-state concentration","Leave Css unchanged","Increase clearance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A larger dosing interval in the denominator decreases average steady-state concentration. — PK I & II, Slide 75, 81"},{"q":"For a drug with 25% oral bioavailability, how much of a 160 mg dose reaches systemic circulation?","choices":["Approximately 4 mg","Approximately 40 mg","Approximately 120 mg","Approximately 160 mg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. 160 mg × 0.25 = 40 mg reaches systemic circulation (propranolol example). — PK I & II, Slide 88-89"},{"q":"Which fraction of a protein-bound drug is pharmacologically active?","choices":["The unbound (free) fraction","The bound fraction","Both equally","Only the metabolized fraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Only the unbound (free) fraction is pharmacologically active and available to act at the site of effect. — PK I & II, Slide 90"},{"q":"Major classes of antimicrobials include cell wall inhibitors, protein synthesis inhibitors, and:","choices":["Inhibitors of carbonic anhydrase activity","Beta-adrenergic blockers","Proton pump inhibitors","Inhibitors of nucleic acid function or synthesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The nucleic acid inhibitor class (fluoroquinolones, metronidazole) is a major antimicrobial category alongside cell wall and protein synthesis inhibitors. — Antimicrobials, Slide 27, 57"},{"q":"Beta-lactam antibiotics exert their bactericidal effect by:","choices":["Binding irreversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit","Inhibiting DNA gyrase","Inactivating penicillin-binding proteins to block cell wall synthesis","Blocking folate synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams inactivate PBPs, the enzymes that complete cell wall cross-linking. — Antimicrobials, Slide 33"},{"q":"A concentration-dependent antibiotic with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect is best exemplified by:","choices":["Penicillin V","Vancomycin","A natural first-generation cephalosporin","A fluoroquinolone such as ciprofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones show concentration-dependent killing with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect. — Antimicrobials, Slide 24, 59"},{"q":"The minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) is determined by antibiotic sensitivity testing such as the:","choices":["Gram stain","Coagulase test","E-test","Antibiogram color chart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The E-test is used to determine the MIC of an antibiotic against an organism. — Antimicrobials, Slide 13"}]};
